e vertical; rho has small closed eye; mu has slanting first and final strokes, with the mid-strokes joining as a curve and only descending half-way; alpha is broad with straight bar, in one case slightly curved with the apex extending at the top; nu has shorter right vertical joining the diagonal above the line; omicron is small and mid-line.",[61,67],{"type":62,"l":63,"from":64,"to":64,"h":65,"unit":66,"atLeast":65,"atMost":65},"letterHeight","Line 1","line1","30","mm",{"type":68,"l":69,"from":64,"to":70,"h":71,"unit":66},"interlinear","Interlineation line 1 to 2","line2","NA",{"_":73,"notBefore":74,"notAfter":75,"evidence":76},"The tomb has not been excavated fully, but the cemetery went out of use around the end of the 3rd century BCE or early 2nd century, offering a terminus ante quem",-350,-201,"archaeological-context",[78,84],{"_":79,"type":80,"ref":81,"corresp":82,"region":83},"Abacaenum","ancient","http://pleiades.stoa.org/places/462062","#findspot","Sicilia",{"_":8,"type":85,"ref":86,"offset":87,"region":83},"modern","http://sws.geonames.org/2522853","near",[89],[90,91],38.05191,15.10158,[13,14],"Sicily",{"_":4,"role":3,"ref":5},{"type":96},"inventory",{"_":98,"mainLang":99,"languages":100},"Ancient Greek","grc",[98],[102],{"atLeast":103,"atMost":103},30,[105,106],"Bacci, Coppolino, 2009, La Necropoli di Abakainon: primi dati","Sofia, 2018",[108,109],"DX8AWC9Q","JQQKI6JP",[111,112,113,37,41,45,114,115,116,117,115,118,119,99],"isic004415","epitaph for krimma, child of aunakos",{"_":23},"abacaenum","tripi","sicilia","sicily","necropoli di abakainon","ancient greek","\u003Cdiv class=\"textpart\" lang=\"grc\">\u003Cspan class=\"ab\" id=\"div-interpretive\">  \u003Ca id=\"al1-interpretive\">\u003C!--0-->\u003C/a>\u003Cspan class=\"linenumber\">1\u003C/span>Κριμμα Αυνακου \u003C/span>\u003C/div>  \u003Cp class=\"miniapp\">\u003C/p>",9999999999,{"file":123,"uri":123,"title":124,"status":125,"editions":126,"editionAuthor":35,"type":132,"objectType":133,"material":134,"condition":10,"layoutDesc":136,"handNote":140,"date":146,"places":147,"geo":150,"country":83,"provenance":79,"provenanceGeo":152,"region":93,"settlement":8,"repository":153,"idno":154,"textLang":155,"tmNumber":10,"letterHeights":157,"publications":160,"zotero":161,"keywords":162,"html":166,"idnoSort":121,"languageSort":98,"materialSort":45,"originSort":79,"typeSort":37},"ISic004416","Epitaph for Philon, son Aunakos",{"_":23},[127,128,129,130,131],{"type":26},{"type":28},{"type":30},{"type":32},{"type":34},{"_":37,"ana":38,"ref":39},{"_":41,"ana":42,"ref":43},{"_":45,"ana":46,"type":47,"subtype":135,"ref":50},[49],{"layout":137},{"p":138,"rs":139,"damage":10},"Single line of greek letters approximately centred on the face of the plinth, descending to right",{"_":55,"ana":56,"ref":57},{"lettering":141,"dimensions":142},"Crude and irregularly carved large letters. Phi has a small eye, seemingly formed of two small loops; omega is small, mid-line, open, with outward/downward sloping tails; nu is large and uneven; alpha is broad, but the form of the bar is illegible; kappa has short equal arms joined to the vertical; omicron appears small and midline.",[143,145],{"type":62,"l":63,"from":64,"to":64,"h":144,"unit":66,"atLeast":144,"atMost":144},"59",{"type":68,"l":69,"from":64,"to":70,"h":71,"unit":66},{"_":73,"notBefore":74,"notAfter":75,"evidence":76},[148,149],{"_":79,"type":80,"ref":81,"corresp":82,"region":83},{"_":8,"type":85,"ref":86,"offset":87,"region":83},[151],[90,91],[13,14],{"_":4,"role":3,"ref":5},{"type":96},{"_":98,"mainLang":99,"languages":156},[98],[158],{"atLeast":159,"atMost":159},59,[105,106],[108,109],[163,164,165,37,41,45,114,115,116,117,115,118,119,99],"isic004416","epitaph for philon, son aunakos",{"_":23},"\u003Cdiv class=\"textpart\" lang=\"grc\">\u003Cspan class=\"ab\" id=\"div-interpretive\">  \u003Ca id=\"al1-interpretive\">\u003C!--0-->\u003C/a>\u003Cspan class=\"linenumber\">1\u003C/span>Φίλων Αυ̣νακου \u003C/span>\u003C/div>  \u003Cp cl